<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
	<!--
	Sparkle 2 sandboxing (TECH-SPEC §10, plan U11): the app stays sandboxed
	(app-sandbox above is NOT disabled) and uses Sparkle's Installer XPC
	Service, which is bundled prebuilt inside Sparkle.framework's
	XPCServices/ directory when Sparkle is consumed via SPM/binary
	distribution — no custom Installer.xpc/Downloader.xpc Xcode targets are
	needed for this path (per sparkle-project.org/documentation/sandboxing/
	and github.com/sparkle-project/Sparkle/discussions/2270). The mach-lookup
	temporary exceptions below are what let the sandboxed app talk to that
	bundled installer service; SUEnableInstallerLauncherService=YES in
	Info.plist is the corresponding app-side switch.

	We deliberately do NOT enable Sparkle's Downloader XPC Service
	(SUEnableDownloaderService) or attempt to sandbox it: that requires
	building Sparkle from source and editing its own ConfigCommon.xcconfig
	(DOWNLOADER_SANDBOXED_ENTITLEMENTS / XPC_SERVICE_BUNDLE_ID_PREFIX), which
	is unnecessary here — Downloader sandboxing only matters for apps that
	lack the network client entitlement, and this app already has
	com.apple.security.network.client above, so Sparkle's update-file
	download rides on the app's own sandboxed network access instead.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.temporary-exception.mach-lookup.global-name</key>
	<array>
		<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)-spks</string>
		<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)-spki</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
